		<td colspan=3 bgcolor="#ffffff">
			<h1>Talent and Tenacity: Ka'Ramuu Kush</h1>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td bgcolor="#ffffff"><img src="images/clear_dot.gif" alt="" width=1 height=1></td>
		<td bgcolor="#ffffff">
<p><b>by <a href="#author">Paula Farmer</a></b></p>
<p>Even when&nbsp;Ka&rsquo;Ramuu Kush isn't trying to garner attention or controversy, somehow it manages to find him. This past summer, it occurred at the funeral of National Black Theatre&rsquo;s producer Tunde Samuel. Most of those in attendance were either in traditional black funeral attire or lighter garments, &ldquo;acceptable&rdquo; for the warm New York summer day that it was. Ka&rsquo;Ramuu, determined to be true to himself yet respectful of Tunde, was clad in an unconventional bright African sarong, white tank top and a straw hat. His choice of attire drew criticisms and overshadowed his quiet, yet profound words to honor his friend and mentor. But anyone who knows anything about Tunde knows &ldquo;convention&rdquo; wasn&rsquo;t a part of his vocabulary and Ka&rsquo;Ramuu was just keeping it real. Undaunted, as usual, Ka&rsquo;Ramuu continued Samuel&rsquo;s celebratory send off with enthusiasm.</p>
<p><br />At least one person at the funeral got past the clothes that Kush wore and honed in on the words that came out of his mouth and heart. &ldquo;What was that you said?&rdquo; he asked. &ldquo;Something about the passing of kings?&rdquo; &ldquo;Kings passing give purpose to men&rsquo;s lives,&rdquo; Kush reiterated. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s from a play I wrote.&rdquo;</p>
<p><br />The whole scenario is incredibly reflective of the essence of this young thespian-unconventional, real, un-daunted. Someone who would say creative and profound things to honor one of many mentors and idols, Kush knows the value of being original and respectful of those that paved the way for him. In addition to Tunde Samuel, he often credits playwright Laurence Holder and veteran actors Avery Brooks and Ossie Davis with inspiring him and making it possible for him to establish a career. </p>
<p><br />While most young men his age are climbing the proverbial corporate ladder, Kush is carving a creative niche for himself throughout New York. He wears many hats at many places, including actor and director for the National Black Theatre (NBT), actor, director and artistic associate for the New Federal Theatre (NFT) and just recently, associate director of the junior scholars program at the Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture. In addition, he also does freelance work as an acting coach as well as work on numerous independent projects at other off Broadway venues. But it&rsquo;s not just about the work he does for others, writing his own screenplays and theater scripts consumes much of his time of late as well.</p>
<a name="author"></a>
<div class="greybox">
	<p><b>Paula Farmer</b> is a New York-based freelance print/broadcast journalist whose work focuses on theatre and film reviews and features. She currently has a segment on Drum Television's "Uptown," a weekly news magazine in Harlem. She recently completed producing and directing her first documentary.</p>
</div>


